  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1145 AM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nEarlier this morning an area of high pressure at the surface \nwas located over the Northern Plains and a cold front was \nlocated across Oklahoma and the Texas Panhandle. Across the \nCentral Plains a northwest flow aloft was present as an upper \nlevel trough moves into the Northern Mississippi Valley and an \nupper level ridge builds across the western United States.\n\nIn the near term temperatures will stay near the seasonal \nnormals for early October as the surface high builds across the \nPlains. This will result in lows tonight around 50 degrees. \nThere is a 20-30% chance for lows overnight to drop into the mid\n40s if skies remain clear north and east of Dodge City. Highs \non Monday are expected to once again climb into at least the mid\n70s.\n\nOn Tuesday the northwest flow aloft will continue as the upper \nridge remains nearly stationary over the western United States. \nWhat cooler air that is located over southwest Kansas on Monday \nwill erode from west to east as the warmer downslope flow air \nspreads east. The 850mb temperatures are forecast to warm into \nthe 20 to 25C range by late day. Based on this warmup and an \nafternoon of sunshine the highs should easily climb back into \nthe 80s as all the models suggest. Also would not be surprised \nif the afternoon temperatures end up being a few degrees warmer \nthan the current forecast given the forecast 850mb temperatures \nand the past trend of guidance being too cool with it high \ntemperature forecast. \n\nAs for the remainder of the work week and early next weekend \nthe upper ridge over the western United States will weaken late \nweek and a more zonal flow will develop across the Rockies. As \nthe westerly downslope flow improves, a trough of low pressure \nat the surface will deepen in eastern Colorado and the southerly\nwinds across western Kansas will increase. Along with the \nstronger winds late week/early next weekend there will also be \nthe potential for deeper mixing to occur which would allow the \nwarmer 850-700mb temperatures to mix down to the surface. As a \nresult, not only is the ongoing forecast with unseasonably warm \ntemperatures across western Kansas late in the week still look \nreasonable (highs in the mid 80s), but would also not be \nsurprised to see the highs being a few degrees higher.",
